# Take More — environment variables
#
# Names only. No values live in this file and none ever should: it is committed,
# and `.env.local` (which is not) is where the real ones go.
#
# `scripts/check-launch-ready.mjs` asserts that every `process.env.X` read
# anywhere in apps/ or packages/ appears below, so adding a variable to the code
# without documenting it here fails CI. That is the point of the file.
#
# Copy to .env.local and fill in:  cp .env.example .env.local

# ── Supabase ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# From the project's API settings. New-format keys (`sb_publishable_…`,
# `sb_secret_…`), not the legacy anon/service_role pair.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
# Bypasses RLS entirely. Server only. Never in a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

# Management API — migrations, type generation and the parity suite. Not needed
# at runtime by either app.
SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF=
SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ── The two apps pointing at each other ─────────────────────────────────────
# The storefront's public URL. NEXT_PUBLIC_ so client components can build links
# with it; the unprefixed one is the server-side fallback.
NEXT_PUBLIC_STOREFRONT_URL=http://localhost:3000
STOREFRONT_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Shared secret ops uses to ask the storefront to revalidate a page, and an
# accepted fallback credential for a manual /api/match run.
REVALIDATE_SECRET=

# ── Email (Resend) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Until RESEND_API_KEY exists, ops sends nothing by email and says so in the UI;
# the one-tap WhatsApp queue still works. Put marketing on its own subdomain so
# a newsletter complaint cannot damage transactional deliverability.
RESEND_API_KEY=
RESEND_MARKETING_FROM="Take More <stock@news.takemoreequipment.co.za>"
RESEND_REPLY_TO=

# The postal address in the footer of every marketing email. A physical address
# is what makes a commercial sender identifiable — POPIA s69 and every major
# mailbox provider's bulk-sender guidance both expect one.
#
# It must agree with contact.legalName and contact.address in
# apps/web/src/data/launch.ts. It is a variable rather than an import because
# the ops app cannot reach the storefront's launch manifest; docs/launch-checklist.md
# lists the two together so they cannot be updated apart.
BUSINESS_POSTAL_IDENTITY="Take More Equipment (Pty) Ltd, Unit 4, 19 6th Rd, Montague Gardens, Cape Town, 7441"

# ── What goes on an invoice ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPS ONLY. Everything the business says about itself on a document a customer
# keeps. None of it is here because none of it may be in the repository: this
# repo is PUBLIC, and a bank account number, while not a secret in the sense the
# key scanner means, is the business's own detail and would be in git history
# for ever.
#
# WITHOUT THE FIRST THREE, NO INVOICE IS ISSUED AT ALL. That is deliberate and
# it is not a nicety:
#
#   * Companies Act s32 requires a company's registered name and registration
#     number on its business documents, and issuing one with a wrong number is
#     an offence rather than a typo.
#   * apps/web/src/data/launch.ts carried 0000/000000/00 as the registration
#     number and an unverified legal name right up until the day invoicing was
#     built. Without this check, the first invoice this system ever issued would
#     have gone out with a placeholder on it, and the first person to notice
#     would have been a customer's bookkeeper. Both were verified with the owner
#     on 2026-08-20; the check stays, because the next deployment starts empty.
#
# Both ends check: issuerFromEnv() in apps/ops/src/lib/invoice.ts refuses to
# assemble one, and public.issue_invoice() refuses to write one.
#
# BUSINESS_LEGAL_NAME must agree with contact.legalName and
# BUSINESS_REGISTRATION_NUMBER with contact.registrationNumber in
# apps/web/src/data/launch.ts. Same pairing as BUSINESS_POSTAL_IDENTITY above,
# and docs/launch-checklist.md lists them together for the same reason.
BUSINESS_LEGAL_NAME=
# CIPC form, YYYY/NNNNNN/NN, read off the registration certificate.
BUSINESS_REGISTRATION_NUMBER=
# The full postal address as it goes on paperwork. Usually the same place as
# BUSINESS_ORIGIN_ADDRESS below, but that one is tuned for geocoding and this
# one is read by a person.
BUSINESS_TRADING_ADDRESS=
BUSINESS_PHONE=
BUSINESS_EMAIL=

# The banking block, for somebody paying by transfer. ALL FOUR OR NONE — a bank
# name with no account number is worse than no block at all, because somebody
# reads it, believes they have what they need, and pays into nothing. The code
# enforces that rather than trusting it.
BUSINESS_BANK_NAME=
BUSINESS_BANK_ACCOUNT_NAME=
BUSINESS_BANK_ACCOUNT_TYPE=
BUSINESS_BANK_ACCOUNT_NUMBER=

# Days a proforma gives somebody to pay. Empty means due on receipt, which is
# how this business actually works — the machine leaves when the money arrives —
# and a default of 30 would quietly extend credit nobody agreed to give.
BUSINESS_INVOICE_TERMS_DAYS=

# ── The nightly stock-match cron ────────────────────────────────────────────
# YOU set this, on the ops project, production scope. Vercel does NOT create it
# for you — that belief cost this job its first three nights: the cron fired at
# 04:00, Vercel sent no Authorization header because there was no secret to put
# in one, /api/match answered 401, and nothing recorded that it had happened.
#
# Vercel reads this variable to build `Authorization: Bearer <value>` on every
# scheduled request, and the route compares it back. Any long random string;
# `openssl rand -hex 32` is fine. Changing it needs a redeploy to take effect.
#
# With neither this nor REVALIDATE_SECRET set, /api/match refuses every caller.
CRON_SECRET=

# ── Bot protection (Cloudflare Turnstile) ───────────────────────────────────
# Free. Both keys are needed on BOTH Vercel projects.
#
# Unset in production, the enquiry form and the staff access-request form both
# refuse — deliberately, so an unprotected form cannot ship silently. Unset
# anywhere else they pass, so local dev and CI keep working.
#
# The values below are Cloudflare's documented always-passes test keys. Fine for
# local work; replace with real ones before the domain is pointed here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=1x00000000000000000000AA
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA

# Any long random string. Salts the hash of a requester's IP so the database
# stores a fingerprint and never an address — an IP is personal information
# under POPIA. Changing it resets the per-IP throttle; that is all it does.
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
ACCESS_REQUEST_IP_PEPPER=

# ── Delivery distance and address suggestions (Google Maps) ─────────────────
# One key, two jobs on the ops order screen: /api/distance measures the drive
# from the warehouse to the customer for the delivery fee, and /api/places
# suggests the address as the salesperson types it. Server-side only, on the OPS
# project — never NEXT_PUBLIC_, because this repo is public and the key is
# billed per request.
#
# RESTRICT IT IN GOOGLE CLOUD TO EXACTLY TWO APIS: Routes API and Places API
# (New). One without the other half-works — the half that is missing returns
# 403 and looks, from the counter, identical to "Google has never heard of that
# address".
#
# Unset, /api/distance answers 503 and /api/places returns nothing: the
# salesperson types the address and the kilometres by hand. That is a supported
# path, not a broken one — the fee is computed from whatever distance is stored
# either way, and the driver knows the road better than the API does.
#
# LOCAL DEV READS A DIFFERENT FILE. `next dev` runs with its cwd inside the app,
# so it loads apps/ops/.env.local — NOT the .env.local beside this file, which
# is what the root-level scripts read. A key that is present in one and absent
# from the other is why the suggestion box once came up empty against a Google
# account that was working perfectly. Put ops-only variables in both.
GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=
# Where deliveries are measured FROM. A plain address string; the Routes API
# geocodes it. Keep it in step with the address customers are given.
BUSINESS_ORIGIN_ADDRESS=

# ── Error monitoring (Sentry) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Entirely optional. With no DSN the SDK never initialises, reportError() falls
# back to console.error, and nothing else changes.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
# Source-map upload at build time only. Absent = maps are not uploaded and the
# build still succeeds, which is what keeps the no-secret CI build green.
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
SENTRY_ORG=
SENTRY_PROJECT=

# ── Test harnesses ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which ops deployment `npm run test:pages` drives. Defaults to localhost:3001.
OPS_URL=http://localhost:3001
